-- HW2 Part 3 -- the course's running counter, exactly as defined in
-- labs/running-example.md: x starts at 0, adds 1 while x < 10, resets to 0 at
-- the cap. Both properties below are TRUE on this file as given.
--
-- Your job (Part 3): plant a bug in next(x) -- change nothing else -- so that
-- the property fails, then read the counterexample NuSMV gives you.
--
-- Why x is declared 0..25 and not 0..10: if the type already stopped x at 10,
-- the property would hold by declaration and there would be nothing to check.
-- The bound has to come from the transition relation.
--
-- One thing that will bite: NuSMV checks the RANGE of every next(x) branch for
-- every declared value of x, reachable or not. If any branch can produce a
-- value outside 0..25 -- say 26 -- the tool stops before checking anything,
-- with   "cannot assign value 26 to variable x".   That is a type error, not a
-- counterexample. Keep your bug's arithmetic inside 0..25.
--
-- Run it:  NuSMV counter.smv    (or nuXmv counter.smv)
--
-- Part 3 needs only that plain check. If you do try `-bmc` on this file, expect
-- "Warning: cannot assign value 26 to variable x" even before you change
-- anything: under BMC it is a warning, not the error above, and the run goes on.

MODULE main
  VAR
    x : 0..25;

  ASSIGN
    init(x) := 0;
    next(x) := case
                 x < 10 : x + 1;   -- the guard
                 TRUE   : 0;       -- the reset
               esac;

  -- The same property twice: as an invariant, and as LTL. Their
  -- counterexamples are shaped differently -- compare them.
  INVARSPEC x <= 10
  LTLSPEC   G (x <= 10)
